 Recent advances in brain-to-language decoding have expanded the field from constrained recognition to text          
generation and streaming personalized speech, synthesizing developments across invasive and non-invasive measurements
while highlighting the need for shared benchmarks and longitudinal studies [^2609.27650]. To address session         
variability in invasive brain-machine interfaces, Task-Conditioned Latent Alignment (TCLA) stabilizes neural decoding
by learning a shared latent space aligned separately for each task condition, significantly reducing failure rates   
across cross-subject and long-term evaluations [^2609.27441]. In the realm of signal preprocessing, attention-guided 
conditional adversarial learning (P2P Trans) effectively suppresses electromyographic artifacts in single-channel    
EEG, demonstrating superior spectral restoration under severe contamination [^2609.26772]. Furthermore, modeling     
long-horizon EEG sequences benefits from neuroscience-inspired tokenization; Brain-Token Learning replaces fixed     
temporal windows with microstate-derived brain tokens, capturing quasi-stable large-scale brain states and improving 
representation learning across affective and clinical datasets [^2609.24324]. Optimizing cross-subject EEG decoding  
remains challenging due to gradient nonstationarity, but adaptive forgetting controllers like AFOR dynamically adjust
second-moment decay coefficients, yielding robust performance improvements over fixed optimizers [^2609.24233].      
Developmental neuroscience also explores these techniques, with proposed frameworks linking infant temperament       
attributes to wakeful resting-state EEG microstates to investigate early self-regulation and emotional reactivity    
[^10.7273_000007929]. Brain-to-image retrieval systems are also evolving rapidly, with NeuroGlyph learning a         
trial-independent visual target across multiple depths of a frozen visual backbone to outperform final-layer         
supervision on THINGS-EEG and THINGS-MEG datasets [^2609.24136]. To address spatial mixing in scalp EEG, adaptive    
cortically constrained alignment methods reconstruct responses into ROI-level source patterns, achieving strong      
zero-shot retrieval performance while providing post hoc interpretability [^2609.24109]. For language decoding,      
Hierarchical Dynamic Neural Decoding (HDND) treats word retrieval as structured refinement, combining intermediate   
neural representations and contextual semantic predictions to yield higher balanced Top-10 estimates across seven EEG
and MEG datasets in multiple languages [^2609.24095]. Auditing EEG foundation models on motor imagery reveals that   
foundation models and task-specific decoders evaluated with matched input pipelines show architecture-dependent      
accuracy differences, highlighting the need for standardized evaluation protocols [^2609.23924]. In clinical         
monitoring, chaotic dynamics-regulated topological learning partitions EEG signals into discrete functional subnets  
and models nodes as Lorenz oscillators to reliably distinguish preictal from interictal states in patient-specific   
networks [^2609.23317]. Large-scale neural recording benchmarks like BrainWideBench demonstrate that pretraining on  
multi-region recordings from hundreds of mice improves cross-animal transfer for decoding behavior and predicting    
masked neural activity, though gains remain highly dependent on objective alignment [^2609.22064]. Furthermore,      
proactive AI assistance systems like PV-Care integrate wearable EEG with visual environmental perception to detect   
cognitive states (Learning, Memory Recall, Resting) in Mild Cognitive Impairment patients, generating context-aware  
for explicit connection-to-connection modeling in rs-fMRI [^2609.23782]. For cellular reconstruction, SomaNet employs
a teacher-student paradigm with affinity learning to achieve accurate, weakly supervised 3D electron microscopy soma 
instance segmentation under partial annotation constraints [^2609.23019]. Training efficiency for latent world models
is also being optimized; Mask-Aware JEPA execution separates mask-independent computation from routing, reducing     
memory traffic and achieving up to 1.7x speedup for brain dynamics and time series training [^2609.22674]. Medical   
imaging synthesis benefits from AWR-Net, which decouples anatomical correspondence from ultrasound appearance using  
wavelet diffusion and residual refinement to generate realistic 3D fetal brain volumes from MRI label maps           
[^2609.22635]. Unsupervised anomaly detection frameworks validated on brain MRI benchmarks achieve strong detection  
performance by inferring corruption parameters as probabilistic anomaly scores, producing estimates of pathology     
intensity and geometry [^2609.21800]. Submillimeter fMRI reliability is enhanced by Voxel-Matching NORDIC, which     
gathers similar non-local voxels based on time-series similarity to boost signal redundancy and significantly improve
temporal SNR without inducing excessive spatial smoothness [^2609.21517]. Accelerated brain MRI acquisition is       
improved by Anatomy-aware Residual Attention Networks that refine SENSE reconstructions through generalized          
self-attention, preserving anatomical fidelity for quantitative measurements [^2609.22390]. Finally, forecasting     
intrathecal tracer enhancement from pre-contrast brain MRI demonstrates that direct image-to-image regression        
outperforms conditional flow matching in accuracy and computational cost, predicting enhancement from anatomy and    
role as an effective treatment option [^10.1227_neuprac.0000000000000284]. Preoperative frailty measures for         
vestibular schwannoma surgeries indicate that tumor size, rather than frailty indices, is the dominant correlate of  
early postoperative outcomes like length of stay and facial nerve function [^10.1227_neuprac.0000000000000289].      
                                                                                                                     
